\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{How the notes are organized}

\p{The developed mathematics follows one practical question: what data must a
move carry so that we can later compose moves and test reachability?
[Incidence, symmetry and moves](frap-0002) answers it in order. First,
[polytopes, flags and facet symmetry](frap-0008) describe the shape and show
their role in triangle and tetrahedron examples. Next, [facet moves](frap-0009)
turn a local facet rotation into an action on incidence positions, with
tetrahedron and cube calculations that make the construction concrete. Only
then do [positions, orientations and global constraints](frap-000A) separate a
piece's destination from transport in its local frame, while [transfer and the
effect of dimension](frap-000B) connect that transport with a group
homomorphism and compute the relevant local abelianizations.}

\p{The cube examples above connect the general mathematics with solving and
play. The later accounts use those concrete states and moves as their starting
point:}

\p{The transition between the abstract facet action and the fixed-frame
cube coordinates is recorded in [how the cube models connect](frap-001A).
Read it before the all-#{N} law if the distinction between incidence
positions, cubie fibres and observations is new.}

\ol{
  \li{\strong{General Rubik puzzles:} the incidence actions, framed
  orientations and transfer developed below.}
  \li{\strong{The [layered-cube family](frap-0003):} piece types, layer
  turns and conventions as #{N} varies.}
  \li{\strong{The [layered-cube law](frap-0004):} all-#{N} state
  coordinates and a source-backed sufficiency sketch for reachability.}
  \li{\strong{The [complete three-layer cube](frap-0016):} explicit outer
  generators, impossible states, the standard count and a worked cleanup.}
  \li{\strong{[Human methods and computer search](frap-0005):}
  recognizable cases, algebraic effects, usability, move costs and optimality.
  The [complete layer-first method](frap-0017) supplies one full human
  construction, and [computer solving and efficiency](frap-0018) supplies
  exact small search, admissible bounds and subgroup reduction.}
  \li{\strong{[From move sequences to play](frap-0006):} notation, scrambling,
  recognition, replay and complete solves, including the [worked replay and
  projected lower bound](frap-001B).}
}

\p{[Further connections](frap-0007) relate the developed orientation and
transfer calculations to [label transport and topology](frap-0014) and
[scrambling and search functions](frap-0015). The [quotient-compatible move
account](frap-0019) gives the criterion for an identification of observations
to inherit a puzzle action. Together these results compare transport,
randomization and information loss around the same concrete move.}

\transclude{frap-001C}
